| **Ashokkumar, Hewitt, Ghezae & Willer, 2026, Nature:** [Predicting social-science experiments](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10742-x) | **4; existing-model inference; experimental archives** | Predicted experimental treatment effects from study descriptions. Offered a potentially reusable instrument for planning behavioural research. |

Two corrections change the practical lesson. Lake–Baroni was two-person work; Centaur was **not** solo research: affordable training rested on extensive collective data assembly. Also, **Thorp (2026, Science)** issued an [expression of concern](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aej2383) concerning Costello and colleagues; their subsequent [correction materials](https://zenodo.org/records/22754984) warrant checking before treating the original estimates as settled.

**Recurring recipes for 4–10 weeks**

- **Old question, controlled miniature:** Lake–Baroni and Vong make a large theoretical dispute experimentally tractable. Match competing explanations on data and resources; test where their predictions differ.
- **Unexpected intervention, general consequence:** Betley and Cloud change something narrow and find something broad. Replication across models, matched controls and mechanism distinguish discovery from an anecdote.
- **AI as scientific instrument:** Centaur and Ashokkumar validate predictions against independently collected observations. The valuable asset is often the evaluation dataset.
- **Search plus independent checking:** FunSearch demonstrates discovery by producing an object whose value survives without trusting the model.

A workable schedule: weeks 1–2 establish prior art and stopping criteria; weeks 3–5 run the decisive experiment; weeks 6–8 attack alternative explanations; weeks 9–10 replicate and write. This assumes existing datasets or timely participant access. It estimates a research cycle, not publication time.

**Four concrete directions**

All titles below are hypotheses, not established findings. Novelty remains **unverified**. These alternatives prioritize scientific publication over immediate fulfillment of internal program execution and certification—conditions 3, 5 and 7.

1. **“Language models reveal an unrecognized constraint on human grammar.”**  
   **8–10 weeks; linguistic discovery.** Extract rules from controlled sentence judgments, then freeze one genuinely unfamiliar prediction. **Decisive experiment:** independent speakers confirm it on unseen words and contexts, while competing linguistic explanations predict differently. Closest art: [Goertzel, Suárez-Madrigal & Yu, 2020, arXiv:2005.12533](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.12533), which guided symbolic grammar learning using transformer probabilities; [Hu, Wilcox, Song, Mahowald & Levy, 2026, TACL](https://aclanthology.org/2026.tacl-1.7/), which establishes why meaning-controlled comparisons matter. Assumption: a discoverable restriction exists. External extraction relaxes condition 2; it buys independently testable linguistic knowledge.

2. **“Learning experience determines neural preferences for human grammars.”**  
   **6–8 weeks; language acquisition.** **Decisive experiment:** cross architecture with training experience under equal data budgets, then predict human learning on withheld miniature languages. Closest art: Lake–Baroni above and [Kallini et al., 2024, ACL](https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.787/), which found GPT-2 learned manipulated “impossible” languages less efficiently. Assumption: controlled exposure separates competing explanations. This defers condition 10, a new architecture, to identify which experience actually matters.

3. **“A small set of counterexamples preserves grammar during self-training.”**  
   **4–6 weeks; learning from generated data.** **Decisive experiment:** compare examples chosen to distinguish competing rules against equal-sized random and rarity-based samples over repeated training generations. Require preservation of unseen grammatical distinctions across two model families. Closest art: Shumailov above; [Guo, Wu & Yiu, 2026, arXiv:2605.23054](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23054), which already studies linguistic change during self-training. Assumption: distinguishing rules adds value beyond preserving rare examples. Deferring condition 10 buys a cheap, sharply falsifiable intervention.

4. **“Three-way interactions explain human relational generalization.”**  
   **6–10 weeks; cognitive modelling.** Three-way interactions represent relationships that depend jointly on three items; this directly tests your SET advantage. **Decisive experiment:** humans and matched networks learn tasks with identical pairwise statistics but different triple relationships; predict withheld human errors and learning curves. Closest art: [Clift, Doryn, Murfet & Wallbridge, 2020, ICLR](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=rkecJ6VFvr), which introduced three-way attention for logical reasoning, and Webb above. Assumption: the architecture predicts human behaviour beyond accuracy. This preserves condition 10 and converts an existing engineering result into a scientific test.**Pilot direction 2 first: identify the experiences that produce grammatical generalization. Direction 1 has the broadest headline; direction 3 offers a plausible Nature Human Behaviour route.** The strongest opportunity is a new explanation of human language, tested through interventions.

The frontier, verified through **1 November 2026**:

- **Acquisition.** *Poverty of the stimulus* means that children’s experience allegedly cannot explain their grammatical knowledge; the decisive issue is which information suffices. [Vong, Wang, Orhan & Lake (2024, Science)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38300999/) learned word–referent mappings from 61 hours of one child’s recordings. [Charpentier et al. (2025, BabyLM Workshop)](https://aclanthology.org/2025.babylm-main.28/) extended training on child-scale word budgets to interaction and human-likeness evaluations. [Yang, Bisazza, Schneider & Wilcox (2026, arXiv:2602.09992)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09992) found generalization without direct examples at 10M words, but weaker learning efficiency than children.

- **Universals and impossible languages.** *Typology* compares languages; *universals* are proposed shared constraints or tendencies. Their explanation could distinguish biological restrictions from learning and cultural pressures. [Kallini, Papadimitriou, Futrell, Mahowald & Potts (2024, ACL)](https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.787/) found slower learning of manipulated “impossible” English. [Xu, Kuribayashi, Oseki, Cotterell & Warstadt (2026, TACL)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.tacl-1.27/) extended preferences to subtle English/Japanese word-order manipulations. [Ziv, Lan & Chemla (2026, EACL)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.eacl-long.249/) found no systematic possible/impossible separation across a wider sample. Unattested does not establish unlearnable.

- **Emergent syntax.** [Yao, Boguraev, Pimentel & Mahowald (2026, EMNLP)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.emnlp-main.732/) found agreement mechanisms reorganizing after behavior stabilized. [Boguraev, Nakai, Mahowald & Steuer (2026, arXiv:2608.28924)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.28924) transferred grammatical mechanisms across languages, with stronger transfer between similar languages. Finding another grammatical circuit is insufficient novelty.

- **LLM informants.** An *informant* supplies judgments used to infer grammar; reliability determines whether discoveries concern people or model artifacts. [Hu, Wilcox, Song, Mahowald & Levy (2026, TACL)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.tacl-1.7/) explained why meaning-matched sentence contrasts outperform absolute probabilities. [Winston & Kolter (2026, ICML)](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/winston26a.html) trained tiny models on grammatical nonsense, separating syntactic competence from world knowledge. Meaning control is now an experimental variable.

- **Psycholinguistics.** *Surprisal* measures how unexpected a word is; it connects model probabilities to reading difficulty. [Oh, Zhu & Schuler (2025, Findings ACL)](https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.91/) showed that larger models’ poorer reading-time predictions persist without substantial training/test overlap. [Timkey, Huang, Oh, Prasad, Arehalli, Linzen & Dillon (2026, PNAS)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13462876/) separated early prediction-related costs from additional rereading costs in 368 participants. “Prediction is insufficient” is already published.

- **Neuroscience.** *Brain–LM alignment* means predicting brain activity from model representations; it does not establish identical computations. [AlKhamissi et al. (2025, EMNLP; arXiv:2503.01830)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.01830) found alignment tracking grammatical competence more closely than reasoning. [Cai, Kfir, Jamali et al. (2026, Nature)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10691-5) mapped linguistic information in individual neurons during speech production. Crucially, [Hadidi, Feghhi, Song, Blank & Kao (2026, Nature Communications)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-72253-7) showed that data splitting, word rate and positional signals can produce misleading alignment.

These are **cognitive-science experiments** using controlled learning, behavioral testing and model-guided brain experiments. **Assumption:** publication takes priority over immediately satisfying conditions 5/10/11—certification, a new architecture and a browser demo. Existing architectures isolate explanatory variables; inventing an architecture could satisfy condition 10 but adds confounds. Full human studies relax condition 12’s week/$2k limit. Costs below are planning estimates, excluding salary.

1. **“Language universals emerge from failures of cultural transmission.”**

   **Question:** Are absent grammars unlearnable, or learnable but unstable when passed between speakers?

   **Decisive experiment:** Train fresh, open GPT-2-style models on matched miniature languages; require equal initial comprehension. Cross short/long dependencies with generous/restricted production memory, then transmit generated utterances through five learner generations. Use exact grammar checks. Predict which rules disappear, then test human transmission chains. A reversal caused by memory support would distinguish a production constraint from categorical unlearnability.

   **Most dangerous prior:** [Janarthan, Haley & Goldwater (2026, CoNLL)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.conll-main.24/) already found relatively preserved grammatical judgments but poor long-sentence generation in impossible languages. Novelty requires actual transmission and human intervention.

   **Kill:** disappearance follows initial learning differences, or humans show no predicted memory-dependent reversal.

   **Cost/assumption:** $500–1,500, two-week model pilot; $3–6k and 6–10 weeks for adults. Adult miniature-language learning tests this mechanism, not childhood acquisition. **NHB plausible; Nature possible with convergence across several grammatical families.**

2. **“Indirect experience supplies the missing evidence for grammar.”**

   **Question:** Which ordinary constructions teach a rule never directly demonstrated?

   **Decisive experiment:** Retrain open 30–125M-parameter models on 10M words with direct question-formation evidence removed. Remove candidate sources of indirect evidence, then restore them with controls matched for vocabulary, length and frequency. Freeze the predicted teaching set; test unseen words and constructions, followed by randomized miniature-language learning in people.

   **Most dangerous prior:** Yang et al. (2026), above, already filter direct evidence. [Yao, Misra, Weissweiler & Mahowald (2025, arXiv:2503.20850)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20850) manipulated input to identify direct and indirect sources of grammatical preferences. The advance must be a specific explanatory teaching set with prospective human confirmation.

   **Kill:** restoration fails across five seeds and two architectures, unrelated examples work equally well, or human transfer fails.

   **Cost/assumption:** $500–1,500 and two weeks for models; child validation needs a collaborator, months and roughly $5–15k. **NHB plausible; Nature only with broad developmental confirmation.** Success could refute a specific necessity claim, not all innate contributions.

3. **“A shared repair algorithm explains human and machine sentence comprehension.”**

   **Question:** What computation causes extra difficulty when a sentence forces reinterpretation?

   **Decisive experiment:** Use open Pythia/GPT-2 models to identify representations of competing interpretations. Replace those representations experimentally and derive a compact repair model. Compare it with prediction-only and memory-retrieval accounts. Select new sentences where the accounts disagree; independently match human next-word expectations. Preregister predictions for reading delays and persistent wrong interpretations, then test new participants. Neo’s experience with explicit computational states could make the proposed repair operations inspectable.

   **Most dangerous prior:** Timkey et al. (2026, PNAS), above, already establish the behavioral dissociation. [Barenholtz (2026, arXiv:2606.05346)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.05346) predicts additional reading costs from hidden-state trajectories. Another correlation adds little.

   **Kill:** the repair model fails held-out constructions or offers no prospective advantage over either alternative.

   **Cost/assumption:** $300–800 compute plus $2–4k participants, four–eight weeks. Assumes model interventions yield transferable behavioral predictions. **NHB plausible; Nature unlikely without a broader principle.**

4. **“Human language cortex selects among equally predictive algorithms.”**

   **Question:** Does brain alignment identify grammatical computation or merely shared predictive information?

   **Decisive experiment:** Construct open small models with different experimentally verified grammatical mechanisms, matched on trial-level word probabilities. Fit equally constrained brain-prediction models to public recordings; freeze competing predictions for novel, meaning-controlled sentences. Test these prospectively with a neuroscience collaborator. Model interventions establish causality inside the model; human measurements test correspondence.

   **Most dangerous prior:** [Tuckute et al. (2024, Nature Human Behaviour)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-023-01783-7) already selected sentences that successfully drove or suppressed language-network activity. Novelty requires discriminating mechanisms despite matched predictive performance.

   **Kill:** probability matching fails, differences disappear under confound controls, or prospective brain responses fail to discriminate.

   **Cost/assumption:** $300–1,000 public-data pilot; roughly $10–25k and months for new scanning. Requires a collaborating laboratory. **NHB plausible; Nature a stretch.**

The publication precedents are real but not solo: [Lake & Baroni (2023, Nature)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06668-3) matched human generalization and errors using specially trained networks; [Binz et al. (2025, Nature)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09215-4) built Centaur from a large behavioral dataset and tested broad transfer. Both support ambitious scientific claims through unusually strong human comparisons.## 1. The *Nature* Bar and Brutal Audit of Standalone Assets

Recent cognitive-AI papers in *Nature*—such as **Lake & Baroni (2023, *Nature* 623:115–121)**, which showed a standard sequence-to-sequence network trained via meta-learning across compositional tasks matches human systematic generalization, and **Binz et al. (2025, *Nature* / arXiv:2410.20268)**, which fine-tuned Llama-3.1-70B ("Centaur") on 10M+ human choices across 160 experiments to predict held-out human cognition and align with neural activity—succeeded because they used clean experiments to settle foundational scientific questions about intelligence, not because they pitched an engineering benchmark or a synthetic operator.

Judged against that bar, **none of our five assets can reach *Nature* standalone**:

1. **Higher-order, Newton, Feynman, and Free-Field Attention ([FORMALISM.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/FORMALISM.md), [AUDIT.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/survive/AUDIT.md)) — CANNOT.**
   - *Definitions:* **Newton attention** computes $k$-subset feature products in $O(nKr)$ time using power sums and **Newton's identities** (classical algebraic recursions expressing elementary symmetric polynomials in terms of power sums, avoiding $O(n^k)$ enumeration). **Free-field attention** combines softmax, query-weighted subset attention regularized by a vacuum parameter $\mu$, and a multi-hop matrix walk propagator $(I-uA)^{-1}$. **Feynman attention (`DS-PA`)** iterates a tree-level recursion of pairwise and cubic interactions across shared rounds.
   - *Why it fails the bar:* On natural language (enwik8 bytes and 400M FineWeb-Edu tokens), subset and Newton attention yield zero perplexity gain over softmax (1.652 vs. 1.650 bits/char; 3.714 vs. 3.684 nats/token in [AUDIT.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/survive/AUDIT.md#L38)) at $1.9\text{--}2.6\times$ the wall-clock cost. Natural text lexicalizes multi-way relations through pairwise syntax and hub words ([WHITEBOARD2.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/WHITEBOARD2.md#L35-L40), Law 2). Feynman attention's 62k vs. 154k pointer-chasing win ([AUDIT.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/survive/AUDIT.md#L93-L98)) relies on four weight-tied propagation rounds rather than cubic interaction, and fails completely on Match-4 (50.0%) due to the **statistical-query leap barrier** (**Abbe, Boix-Adsera & Misiakiewicz, 2023, COLT / arXiv:2304.03752** — proved SGD learning time is governed by the lowest nonzero Fourier degree, or "leap," of the target function).
2. **Sketchpad / Savant Certification as a Calculator or Table Executor ([paper.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/sketchpad/paper/paper.md), [META_REPORT.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/META_REPORT.md)) — CANNOT.**
   - *Definitions:* A **pinned-sheet certificate** verifies a model's logit **margin** (the logit gap between the true token and runner-up, bounding numerical drift) on all 2,446 finite local 3-cell views so [`thm-p`](file:///Users/neo/repos/sketchpad/paper/paper.md#L58-L60) guarantees exactness at all lengths.
   - *Why it fails the bar:* The local-view certification principle is owned by **Moore (1956, *Automata Studies*, pp. 129–153)** (proved no finite experiment identifies an unbounded-state machine), **Cai, Shin & Song (2017, ICLR / arXiv:1704.06611)** (proved bounded recursive views reduce unbounded-length neural execution correctness to finite input checks), **Abbe, Bengio, Lotfi, Sandon & Saremi (2024, NeurIPS / arXiv:2406.06467)** (introduced history eviction and positional resetting in the Inductive Scratchpad), **Rodionov & Prokhorenkova (2025, ICML / arXiv:2402.11628)** (proved out-of-distribution correctness for discrete-state neural algorithmic reasoners, DNAR), and **Lewandowski, Machado & Schuurmans (2026, arXiv:2601.08061)** (proved finite rule verification certifies universal $L(U_{15,2})$ computation in Llama-4 decoding). Worse, in [paper.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/sketchpad/paper/paper.md#L38) a Python runtime moves the window and runs Knuth's outer loops, while the dialogue adapter loses 5.8 points on fresh GSM8K ([paper.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/sketchpad/paper/paper.md#L133)) and Savant/Loom loses to its base model on user prompts (0.54 vs. 0.73 in [META_REPORT.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/META_REPORT.md#L48)).
3. **Standalone Nerode Minimal Automata, Holonomy, and the Characters Law ([THEOREMS.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/THEOREMS.md), [GROUPS.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/GROUPS.md)) — CANNOT.**
   - *Definitions:* **Myhill–Nerode equivalence** groups histories that yield identical outputs for all future continuations into the minimal deterministic finite automaton (DFA); **holonomy attention** assigns each token a non-commuting rotation $U_t \in \mathrm{SO}(d)$ to multiply ordered subsets; the **characters law** states that Match-$k$ balanced accuracy scales as $\frac{1}{2} + F/80$ with the number $F$ of learned **group characters** (homomorphisms from a group to roots of unity that diagonalize group convolution).
   - *Why they fail standalone:* Recovering a 960-state minimal automaton in a ~20D affine subspace ([r8_status.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/rounds/r8_status.md#L43-L45)) is classical **Hankel-matrix spectral realization** (**Hsu, Kakade & Zhang, 2009, COLT / arXiv:0811.4413** — proved SVD of the past-by-future probability matrix recovers minimal linear state spaces), and our robust cell theorem ([`robustMyhillNerode_full`](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/THEOREMS.md#L514)) was scooped by **Brandoit et al. (2026, arXiv:2609.32447)** (proved an iff invariant-cell contraction theorem for length-independent state tracking). Similarly, learning Fourier characters and **irreducible representations** (minimal invariant matrix subspaces of a group, or irreps) is owned by **Nanda, Chan, Lieberum, Smith & Steinhardt (2023, ICLR / arXiv:2301.05217)** (reverse-engineered modular-addition grokking into discrete Fourier rotations) and **Chughtai, Chan & Nanda (2023, ICML / arXiv:2305.13534)** (showed networks learn group irreps and characters on finite groups), while [AUDIT.md](file:///Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/survive/AUDIT.md#L68-L72) exposed BOS contamination and test-threshold leakage in the characters law.

